# How the parser pairs a bracket's opener with its closer, by the lexer's own
# stack (design.md §4.17; defect 131's class; split out of `parse/unclosed`
# on 2026-10-01, the recovery batches' C3, when the pairing came to read the
# kinds of the openers it walks past).
#
# The lexer keeps every opener on one stack and names the ones it found
# never closed; the parser, reading the same tokens, must pair the same, or
# it would name a closer the lexer already named missing, or read lines the
# lexer closed a bracket before as inside it. So it walks the tokens from an
# opener as the lexer walked them, and where the lexer named what was open,
# at a declaration's line, at a reach's end, at a lost quote's line end or at
# a closer of another kind (`cursor.Cursor.closes`), it stops where the lexer
# did. A leaf: it reads the tokens and nothing that reads the parser.
#
# | entry | called by |
# |---|---|
# | `paired_closer` | `parse/unclosed.never_closed`, `parse/unclosed.recover_past_pair` |
# | `paired_by_kinds` | `paired_closer`, past a closer of another kind |
# | `close_after` | `parse/unclosed.in_reach`, `parse/brace_habit.closer` |

use token
use cursor
use literals
use lexer

# The index of the closer that closes the opener at token `open`, as the
# lexer's stack pairs them, whatever its kind, or -1 where none does: every
# opener pushed, every closer popping the last one, within the file the
# opener stands in. The author's text ends at `root_end`, where the lexer
# ended its file and named what was left open; and the lexer named every
# opener still open at a declaration opening its line at column 0, at a
# line a reach ends at, and at a line a lost quote ended
# (`cursor.Cursor.closes`), so a closer past one closes nothing before it;
# a closer at such an offset is one of another kind than the innermost,
# which closed the opener of its own kind below and named those above it
# (`closers.hero`, 2026-10-01). A literal that never closed took the closers
# on the rest of its line (`literals.closers_taken`), popped as the lexer
# popped them: an opener one of those closed has no closer the parser can
# take.
function paired_closer(c: cursor.Cursor, text: str, open: i64) -> i64
    depth: i64 @ 0
    i: i64 @ open
    bounded = c.root_end >= 0 && c.tokens[open].span.start < c.root_end
    closed = close_after(c, at: c.tokens[open].span.start)

    while c.tokens[i].kind != .eof && !(bounded && c.tokens[i].span.start >= c.root_end)
        k = c.tokens[i].kind
        at = c.tokens[i].span.start

        if closed >= 0 && at >= closed
            # A closer of another kind the lexer paired below the innermost
            # takes the walk that knows the kinds; anything else there is
            # where the lexer named every opener open.
            if at == closed && token.is_closer(k)
                return paired_by_kinds(c, text, open: open)
            return -1

        if k == .lparen || k == .lbracket || k == .lbrace
            depth @ depth + 1
        else if token.is_closer(k)
            depth @ depth - 1

            if depth == 0
                return i
        else if k == .error
            depth @ depth - literals.closers_taken(text, c.tokens[i].span)

            if depth <= 0
                return -1
        i @ i + 1

    return -1

# The same walk keeping the kind of every opener open, which the walk by
# depth hands over to at the first closer the lexer paired with an opener
# of its own kind below the innermost: up to there the two pair alike, every
# closer popping the last opener, and from there this one pops to the
# closer's own kind. Kept apart because it builds an array the walk by depth,
# taken for every bracket the parser reads, does not.
function paired_by_kinds(c: cursor.Cursor, text: str, open: i64) -> i64
    kinds: [token.TokenKind] @ [c.tokens[open].kind]
    i: i64 @ open + 1
    bounded = c.root_end >= 0 && c.tokens[open].span.start < c.root_end
    closed: i64 @ close_after(c, at: c.tokens[open].span.start)

    while c.tokens[i].kind != .eof && !(bounded && c.tokens[i].span.start >= c.root_end)
        k = c.tokens[i].kind
        at = c.tokens[i].span.start

        if closed >= 0 && at >= closed
            if at > closed || !token.is_closer(k)
                return -1
            own = innermost(kinds, opener: token.opener_of(k))

            if own < 0
                return -1

            if own == 0
                return i
            kinds @ kinds.slice(from: 0, to: own)
            closed @ close_after(c, at: at)
        else if k == .lparen || k == .lbracket || k == .lbrace
            kinds @ kinds.push(k)
        else if token.is_closer(k)
            kinds @ kinds.slice(from: 0, to: kinds.len() - 1)

            if kinds.len() == 0
                return i
        else if k == .error
            taken = literals.closers_taken(text, c.tokens[i].span)

            if taken >= kinds.len()
                return -1
            kinds @ kinds.slice(from: 0, to: kinds.len() - taken)
        i @ i + 1

    return -1

# The index in `kinds`, the openers open in a pairing walk, of the innermost
# `opener`, or -1.
function innermost(kinds: [token.TokenKind], opener: token.TokenKind) -> i64
    at: i64 @ kinds.len() - 1

    while at >= 0 && kinds[at] != opener
        at @ at - 1

    return at

# The first offset past `at` where the lexer named the brackets still open,
# or -1 where it named none there.
function close_after(c: cursor.Cursor, at: i64) -> i64
    for close in c.closes
        if close > at
            return close

    return -1

## Tests

# The recovery batches' C3: a closer of another kind than the innermost
# opener closes the one of its own kind below it, the lexer naming the ones
# above never closed at the closer's offset, and the parser pairs the same.
test "a closer of another kind closes the opener of its own kind, and the one above it stays open"
    text = "f(a, [b, c)\nx = [1, 2)\n"
    out = lexer.lex_one(text)
    assert out.diagnostics.len() == 1
    assert out.diagnostics[0].span.start == 5
    assert out.closes == [10]
    c = cursor.lexed_cursor(tokens: out.tokens, root_end: -1, closes: out.closes, swaps: out.swaps)
    assert paired_closer(c, text, open: 1) == 8
    assert paired_closer(c, text, open: 4) < 0

    # `x = [1, 2)`: no `(` is open, so its `)` closes the `[` as before.
    assert paired_closer(c, text, open: 12) == 16
